    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but even with policy, don't you think there's still an underlying momentum? Like, once the tech is developed and it becomes more efficient than a human worker, the market basically forces the hand. It feels like the technology itself is the driver, and policy is just trying to keep up with a wave that's already moving. Wouldn't you say the \"inevitability\" part is the more grounded reality here?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The technological momentum is undeniably powerful; efficiency gains in automation create strong market incentives that make human labor less cost-effective in many sectors. This force is often described as a structural shift, suggesting powerful, self-reinforcing cycles of innovation. Yet, even if technology *drives* the wave, societies still determine the downstream impacts through regulation and investment in social structures. Policy isn't just catching up; it's actively shaping which technological revolutions are adopted and how their benefits are shared.",
      "source": "target",
